  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An extended stretch of dry and seasonable weather \ncontinues through the rest of this week\n\n330 AM Update...\nSurface Temp/Dewpoint spreads of a few to several degrees were\nhelping to greatly minimize the development of valley fog so far \nearly today. \n\nAside from an ill-defined, weak cold front moving SE from the\nLower Glakes region with nothing more than some shallow sct-bkn\nstrato cu (topped by some areas of thin cirrus) across the NW \nMtns during the second half of this morning, skies will stay\nclear under persistent high pressure over the Northeast U.S. \n\nThis dominant weather feature will continue our stretch of dry\nweather with seasonable temperatures for the rest of this week.\n\nTemperatures to start the day today will range from near 30 \ndegrees throughout the perennial cold spots in Central and \nNorthern PA to the mid 30s and lower 40s in the south.\n\nPatchy frost will be possible in the coldest spots across the\nLower Susquehanna Valley where Lancaster and Lebanon Counties\nare still hanging onto the growing season based on the absence\nof widespread sub-freezing temps so far. \n\nHigh temperatures will peak today in most places with mid\nafternoon readings ranging from the low 60s across the highest\nterrain in Northern PA to near 70 degrees in some of the\nSouthern Valleys \n\nLatest medium range model guidance indicates that the dry spell\nwill continue into the final week of October. This rain- free \npattern will be highlighted by a string of sunny and seasonably\nwarm afternoons with low humidity followed by clear and chilly \nmoonlit nights with areas of frost.\n\nAnother dry cold front moving through Wed night/early Thursday\nwill be followed by a moderately gusty northerly wind (in the \n20s - mph) on Thursday.",
